.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * This analysis tests the hypothesis that having daughters leads to a
>  larger increase in the support of gender equality for men than for women (wi
> th gender equality measured by attitudes).
.         
.         * The dependent variable is plh0301, which captures agreement with th
> e statement: “Women should rather care about family than career.” The variabl
> e is reverse-coded, such that higher values indicate stronger disagreement wi
> th the statement and thus greater support for women’s career rights.
.         
.         * The key independent variable is d_daughter, a dummy equal to 1 if t
> he respondent has at least one daughter, and 0 otherwise.
. 
.         * The regression is estimated using a linear OLS model, with the samp
> le restricted to the 2012 wave, which is the only year that includes both the
>  dependent variable and the daughter indicator.
. 
.         * Control variables include respondent gender (sex), year of birth (g
> ebjahr), marital status (pgfamstd), and years of education (pgbilzeit), to ac
> count for demographic and socioeconomic differences in gender attitudes.
. 
.         * Expected finding: If having daughters increases gender equality in 
> the household, we expect a a negative and significant interaction term, indic
> ating that the effect is smaller among women.
. 
. 
.         
.         *H4:
.         reg plh0301 i.d_daughter##i.sex gebjahr i.pgfamstd pgbilzeit if (plh0
> 301 != -8) & (plh0301 != -5) & (plh0301 != -1) & (pgfamstd != -3) & (pgfamstd
>  != -1) & (pgbilzeit != -2) & (pgbilzeit != -1), r

